---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ChangeHistory, { type ChangeEntry } from '../components/ChangeHistory.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema, SITE_URL } from '../utils/schema';

// Terms for using the website. Client engagements run on a separate written
// agreement, which wins on any conflict (see "Working together"). When you change
// these terms, add a CHANGE_HISTORY entry in the same PR (newest first, with a
// one-line summary and the detail); "Last updated" comes from the newest entry.
const CHANGE_HISTORY: ChangeEntry[] = [
  {
    date: 'October 30, 2026',
    summary: 'First published',
    details: 'First version of these terms, covering use of the website, purchases made through it, and how they relate to client agreements.',
  },
];
const LAST_UPDATED = CHANGE_HISTORY[0].date;
const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'jeremy@buildwithjeremy.com';

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: SITE_URL },
    { name: 'Terms of Service', url: `${SITE_URL}/terms/` },
  ]),
];
---

<Layout
  title="Terms of Service | Build with Jeremy"
  description="The terms for using buildwithjeremy.com and buying through it, and how they relate to client agreements."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="theme-bg-primary py-16 lg:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold theme-text-primary">Terms of Service</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 theme-text-secondary">Last updated: {LAST_UPDATED}</p>

      <div class="prose mt-10 max-w-none">
        <p>
          These terms cover your use of buildwithjeremy.com. Build with Jeremy is my consulting
          business; I'm Jeremy Pittman, based in California, USA ("I", "me", "Build with Jeremy"). By
          using the site you agree to these terms. If you don't agree, please don't use the site.
        </p>

        <h2>Working together</h2>
        <p>
          The site describes my services. It is not itself an agreement to provide them. When we work
          together, the scope, price, payment terms, and responsibilities are set out in a written
          proposal or agreement that we both accept. If that agreement and these terms ever disagree,
          the agreement wins.
        </p>

        <h2>Purchases on this site</h2>
        <p>
          Some packages can be bought directly through the site. Payment is handled by Stripe's secure
          checkout, and Stripe's terms apply to the payment itself. What a package includes is
          described on its page and confirmed in the onboarding that follows. Prices and packages can
          change, but a change never affects something you have already paid for. If something isn't
          right with a purchase, email me and we will sort it out; any refund terms in your onboarding
          agreement apply.
        </p>

        <h2>Information on the site</h2>
        <p>
          Articles, guides, examples, and estimates on this site are general information, not
          professional advice for your situation, and results described for other businesses are not a
          promise of the same results for yours. I work to keep everything accurate and current, but
          things change, so check anything important with me before relying on it.
        </p>

        <h2>Using the site</h2>
        <p>You agree not to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>use the site for anything unlawful or to harm anyone;</li>
          <li>try to break into, overload, or disrupt the site or the services behind it;</li>
          <li>scrape or copy the site in bulk, or use automated tools to submit forms;</li>
          <li>submit false information or someone else's details without their permission.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Content and ownership</h2>
        <p>
          The site's text, graphics, videos, and code belong to Build with Jeremy or its licensors.
          You're welcome to link to any page and to quote short passages with credit and a link back.
          Copying larger portions, or using the content commercially, needs my written permission.
          Ownership of work I create for clients is set by our agreement, not by these terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you send me ideas or feedback, I may use them without owing you anything. Anything you
          send that includes personal information is handled under the
          <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Other services and links</h2>
        <p>
          The site uses and links to services run by others, such as Calendly for booking and Stripe
          for payments, along with sites like buildmysystem.co, LinkedIn, X, YouTube, and Upwork. Their
          own terms apply when you use them, and I'm not responsible for their content or how they
          work.
        </p>

        <h2>No warranty</h2>
        <p>
          The site is provided "as is" and "as available". To the extent the law allows, I make no
          warranties about it, including that it will always be available, error-free, or fit for a
          particular purpose.
        </p>

        <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
        <p>
          To the extent the law allows, I'm not liable for indirect, incidental, special, or
          consequential losses, such as lost profits or data, arising from your use of the site. My
          total liability for any claim about the site or a purchase made through it is limited to the
          greater of what you paid me through the site in the 12 months before the claim or one hundred
          US dollars. Some places don't allow these limits, so they may not all apply to you. Client
          engagements follow the liability terms in our agreement.
        </p>

        <h2>Indemnity</h2>
        <p>
          If you misuse the site or break these terms and someone brings a claim against me because of
          it, you agree to cover the reasonable costs of that claim.
        </p>

        <h2>Disputes</h2>
        <p>
          If something goes wrong, email me first and give me 30 days to try to put it right; most
          problems are solved that way. These terms are governed by the laws of the State of
          California, and any dispute that can't be settled informally will be handled in the state or
          federal courts located in California.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
        <p>
          When I change these terms I update the date at the top of this page and add an entry to the
          change history below. The terms in place when you use the site are the ones that apply.
        </p>

        <h2>General</h2>
        <p>
          If any part of these terms can't be enforced, the rest still applies. Not enforcing a term
          right away doesn't mean I've given it up. These terms and the Privacy Policy are the whole
          agreement about using the site; a signed client agreement covers our work together.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about these terms: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ChangeHistory entries={CHANGE_HISTORY} />
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
